","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":91,"newStart":1,"newLines":56,"lines":[" {"proposals": [","   {","-    "id": "rowStep_agree_succ_two_iff",","-    "lean_name": "rowStep_agree_succ_two_iff",","-    "statement": "theorem rowStep_agree_succ_two_iff (n x y : ℕ)\n    (h : x % 2 ^ (n + 1) = y % 2 ^ (n + 1)) (hb : x.testBit n = true) :\n    rowStep x % 2 ^ (n + 3) = rowStep y % 2 ^ (n + 3) ↔\n      (x.testBit (n + 1) || x.testBit (n + 2)) = (y.testBit (n + 1) || y.testBit (n + 2)) := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "THE FREE LEVEL, CHARACTERISED. After the board's own reductions the onset wall is: the agreement width between two rows reaches k+1 by time 2k. The only growth law the board owns is rowNat_return_succ_iff -- the width rises by one when the control bit is black -- and that law alone cannot reach the wall, by measurement rather than by feel. Measured over the seed's own rows at shift p = 16 (explorer/tessera_ladder.mjs, 5000 consecutive steps, width = the lowest bit where rows t and t+16 disagree): the control bit is black on 2022 of those 5000 steps, so a ladder counting only the +1 law gains width at slope 2.473, while the wall's budget is slope 2 -- over budget, so a reset-only ladder is not merely critical, it fails. The width actually gained is 3796, slope 1.317, because 1353 of the 2022 increments are jumps of two or more. This theorem is exactly when a jump of two happens. Once the control bit is black, bit n+2 of both successors is the negation of (bit n+1 OR bit n+2), so the two rows agree there as soon as those two ORs agree -- and an OR forgets information, so it agrees more often than a bit does (5/8 against 1/2 on uniform bits, measured). That coarsening is the free level a wall proof has to price, and it is Gnomon's 'a node whose loop is intact can still be forced, because its two drivers agree' (crystal 62) written as an iff. It strictly generalises the closed rowNat_return_succ_two, which is the single case where both rows are black at