)) ≤ (d : ℝ) * (ε * ((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ))) := by\n        rw [div_le_iff₀ hpos] at hinv\n        nlinarith\n      linarith\n    exact le_of_mul_le_mul_left hz' hpos\n  push_cast\n  rw [abs_le]\n  constructor\n  · have := key _ u2\n    linarith\n  · have := key _ u1\n    linarith",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Prize", "Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnDensityTendstoHalfIffExcess", "Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnCountSandwich", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "rowStep_agree_forward",","-    "lean_name": "rowStep_agree_forward",","-    "statement": "theorem rowStep_agree_forward (n t x y : ℕ) (h : x % 2 ^ n = y % 2 ^ n) :\n    rowStep^[t] x % 2 ^ n = rowStep^[t] y % 2 ^ n := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "AGREEMENT IS NEVER LOST, FOR EVERY START. rowNat_agree_forward says this about the seed's rows and is the reason an agreement front is monotone in time -- the wall's own description names it as one of the three blocks a decomposition should use. The all-starts route cannot cite it, for the same reason as the node above: none of its numbers is a rowNat. Every frontier argument opens by fixing the width already reached and then arguing about the next bit, and that is only meaningful if the width cannot shrink, so this is the standing hypothesis of the whole family rather than one step in it. There is direct evidence the board wants it public: the one-step congruence behind it, rowStep_mod_two_pow, has been privately re-declared in three separate closed proof files (RowNatModEqIterate, RowNatReturnSuccIff, LeftDiagonalOnsetLeIffRowNatReturn) and the bit-expansion testBit_rowStep in four, so no proof file can cite another's copy. The honest caveat on that count, per the caution in crystals about duplication: these files sit on the same brief chain and the copies may have been carried forward rather than independently derived, so the count is evidence that the fact is needed everywhere, not that four people each thought of it.",","-    "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","+    "id": "centerColumnCount_eq_stepMod_count",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumnCount_eq_stepMod_count",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumnCount_eq_stepMod_count (N : ℕ) :\n    ((Finset.range N).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card =\n      ((Finset.range N).filter fun n =>\n        ((stepMod (n + 1))^[n] (1 % 2 ^ (n + 1))).testBit n = true).card := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The board's one bridge from the automaton to the arithmetic it has actually built machinery for — centerColumn_eq_rowNat_testBit, with rowNat_mod_eq_iterate beneath it — had zero dependents on 2026-09-10, which is the whole complaint of crystal 69. This is P2's side of that bridge: the number of black cells among the first N of the centre column is the number of n < N at which bit n of the n-th iterate of stepMod (n + 1) from 1 is set. Why a proof of centerColumn_density_tendsto_half would cite it: after this rewrite the balance conjecture is a statement about how often a bit of a truncated orbit of one bit-twiddle is set, with no cellular automaton, no cone and no configuration anywhere in it — and every tool the board has for that orbit (rowStep, stepMod, rowNat_agree_forward, stepMod_preperiod_of_return, step_two_mul) is stated in exactly that vocabulary. Any attack on P2 through the packed row opens with this line, and there is currently no way to write it down.",","+    "disclaims": "A change of vocabulary and nothing more: it says nothing about the value of the count. Crystal 69 records why the packed-row tools the board already holds do not simply carry over — they settle a FIXED bit index and this is the moving one, bit n at time n, which outruns the settling front (measured at about 1.25 n).",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have rs_mod : ∀ r m : ℕ, rowStep r % 2 ^ m = rowStep (r % 2 ^ m) % 2 ^ m := by\n    intro r m\n    have tb : ∀ s i : ℕ, (rowStep s).testBit i\n        = xor (decide (2 ≤ i) && s.testBit (i - 2))\n            ((decide (1 ≤ i) && s.testBit (i - 1)) || s.testBit i) := by\n      intro s i\n      have h4 : 4 * s = 2 ^ 2 * s := by norm_num\n      have h2 : 2 * s = 2 ^ 1 * s := by norm_num\n      unfold rowStep\n      rw [h4, h2, Nat.testBit_xor, Nat.testBit_or, Nat.testBit_two_pow_mul,\n        Nat.testBit_two_pow_mul]\n    apply Nat.eq_of_testBit_eq\n    intro i\n    rw [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, tb, tb]\n    by_cases hi : i < m\n    · have h1 : i - 1 < m := by omega\n      have h2 : i - 2 < m := by omega\n      simp [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, hi, h1, h2]\n    · simp [hi]\n  induction t generalizing x y with\n  | zero => simpa using h\n  | succ t ih =>\n    rw [Function.iterate_succ_apply, Function.iterate_succ_apply]\n    exact ih _ _ (by rw [rs_mod, h, ← rs_mod])",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","+      "tactics": "  have key : ∀ n : ℕ,\n      centerColumn n = ((stepMod (n + 1))^[n] (1 % 2 ^ (n + 1))).testBit n := by\n    intro n\n    rw [centerColumn_eq_rowNat_testBit, ← rowNat_mod_eq_iterate, Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow]\n    simp\n  simp only [key]",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnEqRowNatTestBit", "Rule30.Proofs.RowNatModEqIterate", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","     },","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 7).all fun n => (List.range 9).all fun t =>\n  (List.range 60).all fun x => (List.range 60).all fun y =>\n    !(decide (x % 2 ^ n = y % 2 ^ n)) ||\n      decide (rowStep^[t] x % 2 ^ n = rowStep^[t] y % 2 ^ n)",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","-      "range": "n < 7, t < 9, x < 60, y < 60, filtered on the hypothesis. Ran true. Claiming agreement one bit wider than the hypothesis gives returns false."","+      "expression": "(List.range 10).all fun n => decide (centerColumn n = ((stepMod (n + 1))^[n] (1 % 2 ^ (n + 1))).testBit n)",","+      "imports": [],","+      "range": "t < 10"","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "stepMod_iterate_eq_rowStep_mod",","-    "lean_name": "stepMod_iterate_eq_rowStep_mod",","-    "statement": "theorem stepMod_iterate_eq_rowStep_mod (n t x : ℕ) :\n    (stepMod n)^[t] (x % 2 ^ n) = rowStep^[t] x % 2 ^ n := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "THE JOINT BETWEEN THE BOARD'S TWO PACKED-ROW VEHICLES. The board now carries the onset wall in two different currencies and cannot convert between them for an arbitrary start. Its reductions are in stepMod -- leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_stepMod_preperiod, stepMod_preperiod_of_odd, stepMod_preperiod_of_return, stepMod_preperiod_le_of_le_11, stepMod_iterate_two_mul -- while every bitwise law about the frontier is naturally about rowStep, because truncating at n hides the very bits n+1 and n+2 that the two laws above read. rowNat_mod_eq_iterate is the only bridge on the board and it converts only the seed's own orbit, so an all-starts argument that proves its step lemmas in rowStep has no way to hand the result to the reduction that consumes it. This is that handover: the truncated orbit of a truncated start is the untruncated orbit truncated at the end. It is also the precise statement that rule 30's row map is a T-function in the sense crystal 60 uses -- information moves only upward through the bits, so the levels form a tower rather than unrelated finite systems -- which is asserted in the docstring of stepMod and proved nowhere.",","-    "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","+    "id": "centerColumnCount_block",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumnCount_block",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumnCount_block (M k : ℕ) :\n    ((Finset.range (M + k)).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card =\n      ((Finset.range M).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card\n        + ((Finset.range k).filter fun j => centerColumn (M + j) = true).card := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "A proof of the balance conjecture has to be able to name the black count on a STRETCH of the centre column, [M, M + k), and not only on a prefix [0, N) — because every fact anyone can hope to prove about the automaton is local in time, and